From 0944551dc5e33010eb6e0fadec5f42d60afade04 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Tue, 1 Sep 2026 09:13:29 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Make shot size a planned field, not a hope
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Every shot in a real character render came back an extreme close-up,
despite the planner prompt asking to "vary the coverage by mixing wide
establishing frames, medium shots, intimate close-ups". Two reasons,
both structural:

  - As prose it was one clause among six competing demands in the same
    sentence — camera movement, light quality, tactile detail, a
    non-repetition rule, an instruction not to name the character. Shot
    scale is what lost.
  - sceneKeyframePrompt then said only "cinematic composition", while
    its own identity language ("place the exact character from the
    reference") pulls hard toward a face-forward frame, because the
    reference is a portrait.

So shot size is now an enumerated field the planner must return per
scene, clamped like the costume lock is, persisted on the storyboard so
an approved plan renders the coverage it was reviewed with, and stated
to the image model in the terms a camera department would use.

The clamp is not stylistic. A wide shot puts the face at a fraction of
frame height and the lip-sync model crops around it — the same
face-pixel constraint that governs the upload path, arriving here as a
composition rule. A speaking scene can be medium or close and never
wide, enforced in three places: the prompt asks only for the two, the
clamp rewrites a wide it is handed anyway, and normalizeShotSize
rescues a board approved before lip sync was switched on.

Making the field required rather than optional was deliberate: the
typechecker then names every site that builds a plan, including two
storyboard resume paths that would otherwise have silently defaulted
and changed a video's shape after approval.

A plan that omits the field falls back to a rotation rather than one
repeated size, so even a model that ignores it produces something that
cuts.

One existing test pinned the word "coverage" in the prompt. It now pins
the enumerated field instead — the mechanism changed, and the old
assertion was checking for the thing that did not work.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
